    "created_at": "2016-11-29T14:30:30Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-11-29T14:31:25Z",
    "body": "There is an issue that, Gecko implements the ruby model from [CSS Ruby spec](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-ruby-1/), which is more complicated than that in the current HTML spec. The CSS model supports continuous `<rt>` elements as well as `<rtc>` element, which means the algorithm you proposed in #2113 wouldn't work for Gecko.\r\n\r\n[Slightly offtopic: this proposal actually again highlights the defect of HTML's ruby model. This model fails to express words like \"<ruby>振<rt>ふ</rt>り<rt></rt>仮<rt>が</rt>名<rt>な</rt></ruby> → 振り仮名（ふりがな）\" in a reasonable way which has desired behavior on both rendering and plain text. HTML spec should really adopt the CSS Ruby model.]\r\n\r\nThere is also a question that whether the parentheses should be proportional or fullwidth (w3c/csswg-drafts#762). I think for CJK languages, majority of people would prefer either fullwidth parentheses or proportional parentheses with whitespace around. Maybe proportional parentheses are more desirable for other languages? Although it seems to me CJK languages (especially Japanese) are the main user of ruby.\r\n\r\nPersonally I don't like to see the algorithm of `innerText` becomes increasingly complicated. IIUC, it was speced this way for web compatibility, not really because of its distinct functionality (?). And thus I don't think it's worth adding anything to it unless for compatibility reason. I may be wrong about this.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
      "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/whatwg/html/issues/comments/263584759/reactions",
